> Settings may vary on your pressure cooker depending on the model. Please refer to the manufacturer's guide.

Cover the @dried red kidney beans (rajma){260%g} with cold water by 2 inches in a large #pot. Let soak at least ~{8%hours} and up to ~{24%hours}. Drain.

Transfer the beans to the insert of a #5L pressure cooker{}. Add @water{235%ml} and @kosher salt{1%tsp}. Follow the manufacturer's guide for locking the lid and preparing to cook. Set to pressure cook on normal for ~{10%minutes}.

After the pressure-cook cycle is complete, follow the manufacturer's guide for natural release. After ~{5%minutes}, being careful of any remaining steam, unlock and remove the lid. Check to make sure the beans are cooked through and soft. If they're not cooked through, add @water{235%ml} and pressure cook for another ~{1-2%minutes}.

Meanwhile, heat the @vegetable oil{1/4%cup} in a large #skillet or #saucepan over medium-high heat. Once shimmering, add the @cinnamon bark or stick{one 2-inch piece} and cook until fragrant, ~{30 seconds to 1%minute}. Add the @onions{2%medium}(chopped) and cook, stirring and scraping occasionally, until the onions are very soft and golden brown, ~{10-12%minutes}.

Add the @garlic{3%cloves}(grated or mashed) and @ginger{one 2-inch piece}(grated or mashed) and cook until the raw smell goes away, about ~{2%minutes}. Add the @Kashmiri red chile powder{1%tsp}, @garam masala{1/2%tsp}, @sugar{1/2%tsp}, @turmeric powder{1/4%tsp} and @kosher salt{1%tsp}. Cook until the raw smell dissipates, about ~{3%minutes}. Add the @canned whole peeled San Marzano tomatoes{350%g}(hand crushed) and cook until thickened, ~{3-4%minutes}.

Add the beans to the #skillet along with @cooking liquid{180%ml}. Mix well and bring to a simmer over medium heat; simmer until the curry thickens and coats and flavours the beans, about ~{5%minutes}. Add more water if needed. Season with @kosher salt to taste. Garnish with @fresh coriander leaves and serve.